```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assign.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Observe.Value("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            Observe.Value("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            Observe.Value("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            Observe.Value("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            Observe.Value("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Observe.Value("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            Observe.Value("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            Observe.Value("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        
            // Debug.Assert(New_Ret >-1, "If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same")
        }
    }
}

```--------------------This is a code that checks the contract of the `LastIndexOf` method of ArrayList collection in C# programming language. It uses `Debug.Assert` calls to make sure the conditions are met after the method is called.

Here is a solution:

```csharp
// @@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

// Check the conditions using Debug.Assert()
Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1 ? Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount : New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1);

// If the return value is non-negative, index of old and new list should be the same 
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 ? New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 : New_arrListIndexOfX == New_Ret); 

// After the operation, list should have the same size as before operation  
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);  

// After the operation, the list should contains the value x if and only if it was
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);  
```
Please note that the solution assumes that the ArrayList class under test behaves as expected and passes only the appropriate tests. In practice, you should also cover all execution paths.--------------------```cs

using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);

	    Debug.Assert(((New_Ret != -1) && (Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount)));
        }
    }
}

```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret > -1, "If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same ");
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "Size of old and new list is same ");
`
```
Explanation:
In method PUT_LastIndexOfContract, we're checking if the `LastIndexOf` method is effective or not, which returns a negative number if not found. If the return value is non-negative, we're checking if the size of `arrList` is still the same before and after adding `x`. So we have written two `Debug.Assert` statements.--------------------The C# code corresponding to the comments would be as follows. Assume variable names in the code are replaced by underscored names to maintain the same structure:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrList_Count = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrList_IndexOf_x = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrList_Contains_x = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrList_Count = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrList_IndexOf_x = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrList_Contains_x = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? Old_arrList_Count == New_arrList_Count : true);
            
	    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
        }
    }
}

```

In this code, the `Debug.Assert` statement checks that if the return value `New_Ret` is non-negative, then the lists count after calling the `LastIndexOf` (not the original list) should remain the same. If the return value is negative, the size of the list does not change.--------------------The statement after the "// @@@" is simply a comment, nothing more. To convert that into a C# Debug.Assert statement, you would add the following lines of code:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 || Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```
This would ensure that the count of the array list remains the same in both the old and the new state of the list if `LastIndexOf()` method returns a non-negative value. Please take into account that the C# Debug.Assert is not a true assertion, it is there for debugging purpose and it will not have any impact on release code.

Similarly, for the below two assertions, you may create a corresponding Debug.Assert statements as follows:

```cs
// @@@ If the indexof(x) returns the same value in both old and new state
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the contains(x) returns the same value in both old and new state
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
